{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target answers the cross-city mechanism question with city size (diameter/total area) and argues the gap scales with urban extent because larger cities have more total thermal storage and anthropogenic heat. But nocturnal UHI intensity is an intensive temperature difference, not an extensive quantity: a city's total stored energy reservoir scales with volume/area, yet the air temperature at any point depends on the local balance between stored-heat release and nocturnal longwave loss per unit area, which is governed by surface properties (thermal admittance, impervious fraction, sky view factor) and is independent of how much total area surrounds it. The target itself acknowledges in its counterconditions that if local urban density/SVF rather than total extent determines the gap, city size is merely a proxy. The standard urban climate literature identifies sky view factor (street canyon geometry reducing nocturnal longwave radiative cooling) as the dominant cross-city modulator of nocturnal UHI, so the target's 'single best' claim is wrong: a compact dense small city can exhibit a larger nocturnal gap than a sprawling low-density large city, directly contradicting the claimed scaling with diameter.","counterexample":["A dense, compact city of 5 km diameter with deep street canyons and low SVF can have a nocturnal UHI of 6-7 K, while a sprawling, low-density city of 30 km diameter with wide streets and high SVF may have a nocturnal UHI of only 2-3 K, contradicting the claim that the gap scales with city diameter."],"target":"14b273a55fe29dbd44eab191266ae140b6cbe4e540060a3c6ae49610e0191fec"}]}